{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgrPsyMY5QYAcTGKRYWXz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UY7f5p2wtTgPxaRrjFRnPR","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"candidates":[{"file":"src/users/infrastructure/persistence/relational/mappers/user.mapper.ts","symbol":"UserMapper.toPersistence","start_line":40,"end_line":75,"explanation":"On a partial update, the repository merges the existing domain user with the payload and maps it back with toPersistence. If the payload has no `photo`, the mapper must leave the photo undefined so TypeORM keeps the existing relation. If it turns a missing photo into `null` (for example an unconditional else branch, or a null default), saving the entity clears the photo FK. That would explain the avatar vanishing after a first-name-only edit. Only an explicit `photo === null` should clear it."},{"file":"src/users/infrastructure/persistence/document/mappers/user.mapper.ts","symbol":"UserMapper.toPersistence","start_line":40,"end_line":75,"explanation":"This is the same risk in the MongoDB persistence path. If the mapper always assigns `photo`, using null or undefined when the payload has none, the `$set` in findOneAndUpdate overwrites the stored photo with an empty value when only the first name changes."},{"file":"src/users/users.service.ts","symbol":"UsersService.update","start_line":120,"end_line":180,"explanation":"The service prepares the payload before calling the repository. It should only resolve and replace `photo` when `photo` was supplied. If it defaults `photo` to null or undefined when absent, or doesn't preserve the existing photo, a partial update wipes the avatar."}],"fix":{"file":"src/users/infrastructure/persistence/relational/mappers/user.mapper.ts","description":"In toPersistence, only set `photo` when the domain entity has one, and set it to null only when `domainEntity.photo === null` explicitly. Otherwise leave it undefined so the existing re